Introduction: 
This project servers a purpose for an overall pratice of various in-need skills. I will be handling a project that is designed to gather weather reading from an external api source and display them. These values are further sent to an ML model that will determine wheather the user will need tot carrya n umbrella for the day. It my be simple in action, but this project will implement fastAPI, Dockers and basic XGBoost Classification Tress for ML.

Structure of JSON Packages:
data(JSON dictionary version of the response)
│
├── location      (dict)
├── current       (dict)
└── forecast      (dict)
        │
        └── forecastday  (list)
                │
                ├── day 1 (dict) 
                |    │
                |    ├── date (dict)
                |    ├── date_epoch (dict)
                |    ├── day (dict) 
                |    |    └── ['maxtemp_c', 'mintemp_c', 'avgtemp_c', 'maxwind_kph', 'totalprecip_mm', 'avghumidity', 'daily_will_it_rain', 'daily_chance_of_rain']
                |    └── hour(list)
                |         └──['time_epoch','time', 'temp_c', 'is_day', 'wind_kph', 'pressure_mb', 'precip_mm', 'humidity', 'dewpoint_c', 'will_it_rain', 'gust_kph']
                |    
                ├── day 2 (dict)
                └── day 3 (dict) 

Features of Files:
weatherapi.py -> responsible for connection with the weatherapi.
main.py -> this file will be used for the final system integration.
database.py -> this file will be used to create table and add row and columns to it (manipulation of the database), also for the insertion of the accurate hourly data obtained from the WeatherAPI calls into the hourly_data.db. Also houses the Recovery of the whole system- returns missed hours and inserts intot he hourly_data.db.
data -> a folder that willl hold the types of data that will be used during th etimeframe of the project
hourly_data.db -> this file will hold all the raw data that will be collected hourly.
feature_eng.py -> this file will contain all the mathematics and dataframe manipulation that will result in the final dataset that will be fed into the ML model.
model -> folder that holds the trained ML model information.
rain_classifier.json -> this file contains the information of the externally trained and save XGBoost classifier ML model that will used inthe project.
predict.py -> this file will be used to call the feature engineering pipeline and run the the ML model.

Feature Engineering:
The process will include converting the database(.db) to a dataframe using pandas. This will let us perform mathematical operations to simplify the ML prediction. 
Later, hourly data had been aggregated based on minimum, maximum and avaeage according to the date feature. This action resulted in formation of 20 columns from the previous 11 columns. 
The following are the columns obtained- 
('temp_c','min'), ('temp_c','max'), ('temp_c','mean'), ('humidity','min'), ('humidity','max'),('humidity','mean'), ('dewpoint_c','min'), ('dewpoint_c','max'), ('dewpoint_c','mean'), ('pressure_mb','min'), ('pressure_mb','max'), ('pressure_mb','mean'), ('wind_kph','mean'), ('gust_kph','max'), ('precip_mm','sum'), 
('dT', 'mean'), ('dH', 'mean'), ('dP', 'mean'), ('dayofyear', ''),('month', '')
But the following columns are expected by the peviouslt trained ML model- 
['temperature_min', 'temperature_max', 'temperature_mean', 'relative_humidity_min', 'relative_humidity_max', 'relative_humidity_mean', 'dewpoint_min', 'dewpoint_max', 'dewpoint_mean', 'pressure_min', 'pressure_max', 'pressure_mean', 'wind_speed_mean', 'wind_gust_max', 'rain_mm', 'dT_mean', 'dH_mean', 'dP_mean', 'dayofyear', 'month', 'rain_yesterday', 'rain_last3_days', 'pressure_3day_trend', 'humidity_3day_mean']

The above is the first component of the feature engineering, but the second part of the feature engoneering includes the creation of a daily feature dataset(data/daily_data.db). Here the aggregated data from the dataframe is obtained and converted to SQLite digesteble datatypes (or else BLOB corruption) and is inserted into the new database. The next step would be for this daily_data to be used by the ML model for prediction.

Automation:
This process should include an automated way of triggering the WeatherAPI to make calls (hourly) and retrive the data depending on the current time(database.db) and send it through feature engineering to derive the dependent feature aggregated based on date(on a daily basis) that will be required by the ML model to predict(feature_eng.py). Later, this data will be loaded into a database that maintains the daily data(daily_data). 
Now, this database will be called by the ML model(in predict.py) to make predictions after analysing the daily data. Before the automation, all of the processes are manually called by the main.py.
But before working on the automation part, it is important to build an understanding on what is the component flow pipeline (architecture) that we will be working right now- 
 WeatherAPI
     |
 Data Collection Layer
  store_hourly_data()
      |
  hourly_data.db
      |
Feature Engineering Layer
   save_daily()
      |
  daily_data.db
       |
  Prediction Layer
    predict()


Scheduler:
This project will be using APScheduler. This is due to is ability to be containerised and it feature-rich property. it will housed in the scheduler.py. Here, we will be using an Interval Trigger (frequency - 1 hour), to time the timer. The scheduler will heavily depend ont he Recovery Mechanism- when there is a failure of the system, the hours missing will be skipped and the current hour will be added to the database. And the missing hours will be addedinto the database with the help of the Recovery Mechanism. This plan of using hte Interval Trigger was scraped beacuse a timer that follows the actual timer was more preferred here. 
Hence, we used Cron Trigger. It follows the actual clock more closely and does justice to out hourly updated system. The following is the configurations of the scheduler - (max_instances = 1, coalesce = True, misfire_grace_time = 180)
Here, max_instances -> the number of threads running concurrently at a time for a job.
coalesce -> the missed fires will be dealt with accordingly base on value (True- the mised fires will be merged inot one single execution, False - the missed fires will be executed back to back.)
misfire_grace_time -> defines how late a job is allowed to start if the scheduler was delayed (Exceution Window Contraint).






